tom@soda.berkeley.edu (Tom Holub) writes:
> If you make polypiling affect, say, the top 5 items of the pile, and make
> wands of wishing, say, be unrechargable or have a max of 1 charge, and
> make rings of conflict give monsters, say, a 50% chance each round of
> attacking each other, you still have all the tricks, but you've
> improved the balance of the game immensely.
>  -Tom

We can't be tempted to continuously modify the game to suit the expert's
needs.  Tom is evidently already aware of a lot of the tricks of NetHack.
And therefore, he is bored with the game because he can consistenly win.
We _could_ make the game harder to suit Tom, so that he could continue to
enjoy the game.

However, I think the greater deed would be for Tom to move on to a different
hobby.  *All adventure games get boring when you've utterly mastered them.*

Nethack is already on the verge of being unapproachable to the novice.
Nethack is a very frustrating game when you don't know the tricks.  One of the
most enjoyable aspects of the game is experimenting and learning new tricks.
At the current level of nethack, it is near impossible to the novice, hard and 
challenging for the player, and enjoyable/easy for the expert.  

I think we should keep NetHack at the same level of playability.

